WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:05.333
Two thousand years ago, Greek engineers built a machine so advanced that nothing

00:05.333 --> 00:08.231
like it appeared again for over a thousand years.

00:08.231 --> 00:10.243
This is the Antikythera mechanism.

00:11.143 --> 00:15.985
Built in the second century BC, this hand-cranked bronze device modelled the sky.

00:15.985 --> 00:21.006
One crank turned dozens of gears, predicting eclipses decades ahead and tracking the

00:21.006 --> 00:22.321
ancient Olympic games.

00:23.221 --> 00:27.796
Sponge divers found it in a Roman shipwreck off Antikythera in 1901.

00:27.796 --> 00:33.312
A year later, archaeologist Valerios Stais spotted a gear embedded in the corroded

00:33.312 --> 00:38.828
lump. Decades after, Yale historian Derek de Solla Price used X-rays to reveal its

00:38.828 --> 00:39.904
true complexity.

00:40.804 --> 00:45.112
Inside sit at least thirty bronze gears, one alone carrying two hundred and

00:45.112 --> 00:49.938
twenty-three teeth. Front dials tracked the zodiac and calendar; rear dials followed

00:49.938 --> 00:52.580
eclipse cycles and the moon's irregular orbit.

00:53.480 --> 00:57.958
Nothing this complex reappeared until fourteenth century Europe's astronomical

00:57.958 --> 01:00.943
clocks, roughly one and a half thousand years later.

01:00.943 --> 01:05.363
It forced historians to rethink what ancient engineers were truly capable of.

01:06.263 --> 01:11.289
The Antikythera mechanism remains one of archaeology's great puzzles, still yielding

01:11.289 --> 01:16.197
secrets through modern scanning. It stands as proof that ancient ingenuity ran far

01:16.197 --> 01:17.932
deeper than we ever imagined.
